WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.240
I’m currently finishing up a round of deck&nbsp;
reviews that will be out hopefully sometime soon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:09.200
and it made me think about the different hurdles&nbsp;
a deck can face. Not necessarily the mechanical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:13.760
weaknesses of a deck, but the fundamental&nbsp;
challenges that make a possibly promising&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:19.120
deck dead in the water. So here, I’m going to&nbsp;
go through the four basic fail states of a deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:23.280
the things that go so wrong they can&nbsp;
make it totally unworkable. All four&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:28.080
of these focus around the idea of a deck’s&nbsp;
“plan”, so the natural starting point is:

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:30.720
#1 — Your deck has no plan

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:34.400
This is the first way a deck can fail, and it&nbsp;
simply means that the different elements of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:38.000
the deck just don’t really come together&nbsp;
to form something that makes sense—it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:42.800
just a collection of cards. Now, I should be&nbsp;
clear here—it is absolutely possible to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:47.600
a deck that feels cohesive and has a sense of&nbsp;
direction without specifically thinking of “a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:52.640
plan”. Some commanders or mechanics or card&nbsp;
types just naturally lend themselves well to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:57.200
a focused deck that can win games—if you have&nbsp;
a Jodah the Unifier deck it’s going to be able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:01.680
to create massive boards of legendary creatures,&nbsp;
and if you have a landfall deck it’s going to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:05.760
able to generate a large resource advantage&nbsp;
while putting on some pressure. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:10.640
there are other mechanics where dedicating a deck&nbsp;
to them won’t automatically create something that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:15.200
makes sense. Let’s say you really like the&nbsp;
deathtouch mechanic and you want to build a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:19.600
deck around it. There’s Fynn the Fangbearer, but&nbsp;
you want something that includes black as well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:25.120
so maybe you grab The Serpent Society or Aveline&nbsp;
de Grandpre. You add some deathtouch-supporting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:29.360
cards like Hooded Blightfang, and then drop in a&nbsp;
whole bunch of your favorite deathtouch creatures.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:36.000
Great! But, is this actually a cohesive deck?&nbsp;
It really depends on how we filled out the deck.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:40.880
“Deathtouch creatures” doesn’t really gesture at a&nbsp;
particular gameplay timeline, lategame boardstate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:46.080
or plan for winning, so a deathtouch deck could&nbsp;
have all sorts of stuff in it. Maybe it’s a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:50.320
lot of combat damage to a player effects since&nbsp;
people don’t want to block deathtouch creatures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:53.600
maybe it’s some counter amplifiers to pair&nbsp;
with Aveline’s combat damage effect or The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:57.920
Serpent Society’s ward, maybe it’s some snake&nbsp;
synergies since we’ve got a lot of those,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:02.160
and maybe it’s just piles upon piles of&nbsp;
deathtouch creatures we like. Depending on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:07.520
what we filled our deck with and how dialed&nbsp;
into it we were, the deck might feel great,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:13.440
but it could also feel like an uncoordinated&nbsp;
mass of nothing. This is where plan comes in—a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:17.440
deck should really have an idea of roughly&nbsp;
what specific boardstate it would like to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:23.680
create when and how that boardstate will lead to&nbsp;
victory. If it instead just has a moodboard of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:31.280
the most aesthetic-looking threshold creatures,&nbsp;
it’s going to run into all sorts of problems.

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:33.840
So okay, let’s say you figured things out. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:38.240
know who you are and what you want to&nbsp;
be doing. But what if that want isn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:42.720
turning into reality? This is where we&nbsp;
get to the second failure condition.

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:46.480
#2 — Your deck fails to execute its plan

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:50.080
This is where you know what your deck&nbsp;
wants to be doing, but it just doesn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:55.040
seem to consistently be happening, at least&nbsp;
not on a reasonable timeline. To solve this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.040 --> 00:03:00.240
I need to introduce a deckbuilding concept that&nbsp;
I like to call “widening your on-ramp”. Magic:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:04.880
the Gathering is a game of chance, and you can&nbsp;
think of your gameplan as a big bucket that your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:09.680
deck is randomly firing tennis balls at. Sometimes&nbsp;
you’ll hit, and your desired gameplan will happen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:15.520
on your desired timeline, while other times that&nbsp;
won’t happen—such is life. What this bucket system&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:22.240
represents—randomness—is entirely unavoidable,&nbsp;
but your deckbuilding choices control the size&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:27.200
of the bucket. Missing land drops will make up&nbsp;
some percentage of that miss territory—a large&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:32.240
percentage if your deck is strongly enabled by a&nbsp;
commander that costs four or more mana—so adding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:36.720
lands to a deck that’s low on them can increase&nbsp;
the size of the bucket. Another thing that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:42.480
increases the size of the bucket is trying to keep&nbsp;
your deck’s payoffs lower-costed and more broadly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:48.160
useful. High-cost amplifiers generally increase&nbsp;
your deck’s peak power level, but these typically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:53.280
don’t help you jump into your gameplan, so each&nbsp;
one you add also slightly narrows your deck’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:57.760
on-ramp. All sorts of other cards also might&nbsp;
slightly narrow your deck’s on-ramp, and I’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:02.240
linked a video I made a couple months ago that&nbsp;
goes more into that, but long story short it can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:07.280
go a long way if you make sure that a relatively&nbsp;
wide portion of your cards are helpful early.

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:12.320
The other thing that can widen your on-ramp is&nbsp;
card draw. If your deck isn’t seeming to work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.320 --> 00:04:16.880
my first suggestion will always be to add&nbsp;
more draw and filtering. A lot of times&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:21.280
this will simply solve the problem, because a&nbsp;
ton of EDH decks aren’t running enough draw,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:26.320
but that’s not the only benefit. Adding card draw&nbsp;
helps even if that’s not the main problem with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:31.040
deck because it helps to pick out what cards&nbsp;
are working and what cards aren’t. It’s easy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:35.680
for a card that doesn’t work well to stay in your&nbsp;
deck if you only see it once every four games,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:40.720
but bump that number up to one in three or one&nbsp;
in two and suddenly you’re coming face to face&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:45.760
with its issues a lot more often. The same goes&nbsp;
for cards that feel really good in your deck—if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:50.400
you’ve got something that’s a surprise powerhouse,&nbsp;
drawing it in more games can help that become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:55.760
clear, and you can modulate your deck to play into&nbsp;
its strengths accordingly. As a final note here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.760 --> 00:05:00.320
sometimes the decks you play against might just&nbsp;
be faster than your desired plan can reasonably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:05.040
be. You can optimize as much as you like, but&nbsp;
some ideas just aren’t suited for a particular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:10.400
caliber of gameplan due to a limited pool of cards&nbsp;
or some inherent weaknesses. I might make a video&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:14.960
about those at some point, but for now I’m going&nbsp;
to move past them and assume you’ve succeeded in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.960 --> 00:05:20.080
assembling a deck that has a plan and is able&nbsp;
to execute it. What could go wrong from there?

00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:23.520
#3 — Your plan sucks for other people

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:27.120
Commander is a social format. You’re&nbsp;
playing a game with three other people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:31.200
and you’re generally there to have a fun&nbsp;
time. So, what happens if you pull up with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:36.240
your beautiful new creation and it just bums the&nbsp;
table right out? I wish this was an area where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:40.800
I could just tell you what’s a-okay and what’s&nbsp;
off limits, but really it’s kind of complicated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:46.080
and there are two main variables at play here.&nbsp;
Variable one is who you’re playing with. Some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:50.480
playgroups are going to be a lot more accepting of&nbsp;
spicy decks than others, and generally people who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.480 --> 00:05:54.880
have played the game longer will be on the more&nbsp;
accepting side. It might strike some people as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.880 --> 00:06:00.080
weird that a large group of players are okay with&nbsp;
lockout control and land destruction and the like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.080 --> 00:06:06.640
but it makes more sense when we think about it in&nbsp;
terms of type 1 vs type 2 fun. Type 1 fun is doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:11.280
stuff that feels good in the moment, whereas type&nbsp;
2 fun is about doing stuff that kinda sucks in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:16.720
moment but is rewarding later. Playing a fiddly&nbsp;
midrange pile is type 1 fun, whereas defeating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.720 --> 00:06:21.920
an especially nasty opposing deck or navigating&nbsp;
a perilous situation falls more in the realm of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:28.160
type 2 fun. This might sound like I’m grouping&nbsp;
people into type 2 players and type 1 players,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:32.960
and there will be people who lean toward one or&nbsp;
the other, but ultimately almost all players are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.960 --> 00:06:38.000
going to enjoy a mixture of these two things, in&nbsp;
varying amounts. You’ll find the dino-playing-est&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:42.000
of Timmies getting jazzed at the sight of&nbsp;
some degenerate Zur the Enchanter nonsense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:45.840
and on the other hand you’ll get an old-school&nbsp;
player who doesn’t particularly want to play&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:50.640
against your Kefka deck today. And that’s the&nbsp;
important thing here—whether or not your opponents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:55.360
want to play against a deck isn’t necessarily&nbsp;
an all-or-nothing question. They might be fine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.360 --> 00:06:59.840
playing against a spicy deck every once in a&nbsp;
while, but making a deck that’s more irritating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.840 --> 00:07:04.400
to play against might mean less times when your&nbsp;
opponents want to play against it, and if your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.400 --> 00:07:09.520
goal is to make an everyday default sort of deck&nbsp;
then that could easily constitute a failure.

00:07:09.520 --> 00:07:14.480
The second variable here is your own willingness&nbsp;
to subject your opponents to things. If you know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.480 --> 00:07:17.680
that you’re somebody who will struggle to&nbsp;
enjoy playing your deck if your opponents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.680 --> 00:07:22.960
react negatively to things you do, you’ll want to&nbsp;
avoid some of the grittier tools out there. On the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.960 --> 00:07:27.040
other hand, if you don’t care one bit about your&nbsp;
opponents having fun, you’ll have a much wider&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.040 --> 00:07:31.840
range of stuff you can do. Whether or not that’s&nbsp;
a good thing is another question, but it is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.840 --> 00:07:36.800
true statement. Personally, I’m somewhere in the&nbsp;
middle—I like it when my opponents are having fun,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.800 --> 00:07:41.520
but I also tend to play decks that present a&nbsp;
very specific type of problem to my opponents,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:46.560
and that isn’t always well-received in the&nbsp;
moment. Getting challenged is challenging.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:50.560
Sometimes we end up driving through the outskirts&nbsp;
of frown-town, but I at least try not to stick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.560 --> 00:07:54.640
people in that same sort of difficult&nbsp;
situation for too long. It’s a balance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.640 --> 00:07:59.360
but it’s what I need to do to play the decks&nbsp;
I like to play in social format. So okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.360 --> 00:08:03.200
let’s say your deck has a plan, it’s able to&nbsp;
accomplish that plan, and you can make peace&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.200 --> 00:08:08.400
with whatever the opposing reaction to that&nbsp;
plan is. What else could possibly go wrong?

00:08:08.400 --> 00:08:11.920
#4 — Your plan sucks for you

00:08:11.920 --> 00:08:16.160
Sometimes, a deck just doesn’t live up to&nbsp;
our hopes for it. You imagine a particular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.160 --> 00:08:20.800
play pattern, a particular sequence of actions,&nbsp;
or a particular gamestate, but upon attaining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.800 --> 00:08:25.840
that you realize that it just isn’t that&nbsp;
fun for you. This is a very common issue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.840 --> 00:08:31.200
and to me it’s just a part of figuring out what&nbsp;
it is that you enjoy within the commander format.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.200 --> 00:08:35.920
Some people enjoy finding the weirdest combos&nbsp;
out there, while others do not. Some people like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.920 --> 00:08:40.160
playing an extremely complicated pile of nonsense&nbsp;
where there’s always another possible line,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.160 --> 00:08:45.280
and to other people that sounds like hell. Some&nbsp;
people like making a massive board full of beasts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.280 --> 00:08:49.440
and other people fall asleep playing that&nbsp;
sort of deck. You can take guesses as to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.440 --> 00:08:54.480
what you will or won’t like, but you won’t&nbsp;
know for sure until you’ve tried it out. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.480 --> 00:08:59.520
like the last failure condition, this one&nbsp;
isn’t necessarily all-or-nothing. You might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.520 --> 00:09:03.600
put together a deck that you really like&nbsp;
playing sometimes, but that simply can’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:08.560
be your bread and butter. That’s a failure if you&nbsp;
were looking to build a deck you play with a lot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.560 --> 00:09:13.600
but it might not be so bad if you’re just looking&nbsp;
for variety. It’s also not a crime to get tired&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.600 --> 00:09:19.200
of a deck eventually. If you build a deck that&nbsp;
executes a very cool, very specific gameplan, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.200 --> 00:09:24.080
might get a kick out of playing it that first half&nbsp;
a dozen times, but it might lose its luster after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.080 --> 00:09:28.640
that. If you poured a ton of money into buying&nbsp;
it in paper and blinging it out, that’s gonna&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.640 --> 00:09:34.160
sting a bit. However, a deck like that eventually&nbsp;
falling by the wayside doesn’t take away from it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.160 --> 00:09:39.440
being fun to play in those first handful of games,&nbsp;
doesn’t negate your past enjoyment. The key here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.440 --> 00:09:44.880
is expectations—if you’re building a deck, try&nbsp;
to have an idea of how often you expect to play&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.880 --> 00:09:49.840
it and how long you expect it to stay fresh.&nbsp;
No deck is a failure if you have yourself in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.840 --> 00:09:54.880
the right mindset for it, and that mindset&nbsp;
is something you can build with experience.

00:09:54.880 --> 00:09:59.280
Thanks for watching, and thanks to Archidekt for&nbsp;
supporting the channel. Archidekt’s deck folder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.280 --> 00:10:04.240
system lets me comfortably group my “sometimes”&nbsp;
